id ^{cite13†1 },\nL27:   * cite20†Andri Iona ^{cite13†1 },\nL28:   * cite21†Christiana Kartsonaki ^{cite13†1 },\nL29:   * cite22†Hannah Fry cite23†ORCID: orcid.org/0009-0006-1584-2042†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 },\nL30:   * cite24†Kuang Lin ^{cite13†1 },\nL31:   * cite25†Yiping Chen cite26†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-4973-0296†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 },\nL32:   * cite27†Huaidong Du cite28†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-9814-0049†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 },\nL33:   * cite29†Daniel Avery cite30†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-9823-9575†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 },\nL34:   * cite31†Dan Schmidt-Valle ^{cite13†1 },\nL35:   * cite32†Canqing Yu cite33†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-0019-0014†orcid.org ^{cite34†2 ,cite35†3 ,cite36†4 },\nL36:   * cite37†Dianjianyi Sun cite38†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0003-3651-6693†orcid.org ^{cite34†2 ,cite35†3 ,cite36†4 },\nL37:   * cite39†Jun Lv ^{cite34†2 ,cite35†3 ,cite36†4 },\nL38:   * cite40†Michael Hill cite41†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-3861-6105†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 },\nL39:   * cite42†Liming Li cite43†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-5873-7089†orcid.org ^{cite34†2 ,cite35†3 ,cite36†4 },\nL40:   * cite44†Derrick A. Bennett cite45†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-9170-8447†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 },\nL41:   * cite46†Rory Collins ^{cite13†1 },\nL42:   * cite47†Robin G. Walters cite48†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-9179-0321†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 },\nL43:   * cite49†Robert Clarke cite50†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-9802-8241†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 },\nL44:   * cite51†Iona Y. Millwood cite52†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-0807-0682†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 }^{ cite53†na1 } &\nL45:   * cite54†Zhengming Chen cite55†ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-6423-105X†orcid.org ^{cite13†1 }^{ cite53†na1 }\nL46:   * cite56†On behalf of China Kadoorie Biobank Collaborative Group L47: Show authors\nL48: cite57†Nature Communications volume 16, Article number: 1869 (2025)\nL49: \nL50: cite58†Save article L51: \nL52: cite1†View saved research L53: \nL54:   * 25k Accesses\nL55: \nL56:   * 66 Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * 23 Altmetric\nL59: \nL60:   * cite59†Metrics details L61: ## Abstract\nL62: Proteomics offers unique insights into human biology and drug development, but few studies have directly compared the utility of different proteomics platforms. We measured plasma levels of 2168 proteins in 3976 Chinese adults using both Olink Explore and SomaScan platforms. The correlation of protein levels between platforms was modest (median rho = 0.29), with protein abundance and data quality parameters being key factors influencing correlation.\nL63: For 1694 proteins with one-to-one matched reagents, 765 Olink and 513 SomaScan proteins had cis-pQTLs, including 400 with colocalising cis-pQTLs. Moreover, 1096 Olink and 1429 SomaScan proteins were associated with BMI, while 279 and 154 proteins were associated with risk of ischaemic heart disease, respectively. Addition of Olink and SomaScan proteins to conventional risk factors for ischaemic heart disease improved C-statistics from 0.845 to 0.862 (NRI: 12.2%) and 0.863 (NRI: 16.4%), respectively.\nL64: These results demonstrate the utility of these platforms and could inform the design and interpretation of future studies.\nL65: ### Similar content being viewed by others\nL66: \nL67: cite60†Image†media.springernature.com L68: \nL69: ### cite61†Large-scale plasma proteomics comparisons through genetics and disease associations L70: \nL71: Article Open access 04 October 2023\nL72: \nL73: cite62†Image†media.springernature.com L74: \nL75: ### cite63†Synergistic insights into human health from aptamer- and antibody-based proteomic profiling L76: \nL77: Article Open access 24 November 2021\nL78: \nL79: cite64†Image†media.springernature.com L80: ### cite65†Integrative proteomic analyses across common cardiac diseases yield mechanistic insights and enhanced prediction L81: \nL82: Article Open access 21 November 2024\nL83: ## Introduction\nL84: Proteins play a key role in human health and most drugs target proteins, including enzymes, antibodies, transport, or structural proteins. Measurements of plasma levels of proteins, particularly when combined with genetic and phenotypic information, can help to understand biological mechanisms and disease aetiology, improve disease risk prediction, and evaluate novel protein targets for drug treatment of specific diseases.\nL85: Advances in high-throughput proteomic assays now enable the measurement of thousands of plasma proteins, and their application in population and clinical studies is likely to transform the development of precision medicine.\nL86: The relative or absolute plasma levels of proteins can be measured using different technologies, including mass spectrometry and affinity-based methods. Mass spectrometry methods identify proteins based on peptides following enzymatic digestion and have been widely used for both targeted (focusing on a few hundred pre-defined proteins) and non-targeted (measuring up to 4500 plasma proteins) approaches in clinical and population studies^{cite66†1 ,cite67†2 ,cite68†3 }.\nL87: However, due to the extensive pre-fractionation required^{cite69†4 }, application of mass spectrometry in large-scale population studies remains challenging. In contrast, advances in high-throughput affinity-based protein profiling technology using the antibody-based Olink^{cite70†5 } or the aptamer-based SomaScan^{cite71†6 ,cite72†7 ,cite73†8 } platform have made it possible to measure plasma levels of several thousand different protein markers simultaneously in large numbers of samples.\nL88: The Olink platform measures protein levels using paired antibodies binding a single target protein^{cite70†5 }, while SomaScan employs slow off-rate modified aptamers (SOMAmers) as protein-binding reagents^{cite71†6 ,cite72†7 ,cite73†8 }.\nL89: Both Olink and SomaScan platforms have recently been used in large-scale population studies, helping to identify genetic variants associated with plasma protein levels (i.e. protein quantitative trait loci; pQTLs) and biomarkers of traits (e.g. BMI), diseases, and their progression^{cite74†9 ,cite75†10 ,cite76†11 ,cite77†12 }. The most recent Olink Explore HT and SomaScan platforms now include >5000 and >11000 protein assays, respectively, targeting a large number of overlapping proteins.\nL90: A few small studies have directly compared the analytical performance of different platforms in the same sample, but these typically involved European ancestry populations and reported only modest correlations between protein levels measured by Olink and SomaScan^{cite78†13 ,cite79†14 ,cite80†15 ,cite81†16 }. Moreover, the protein-phenotype associations and pQTLs identified in these studies varied between the different platforms, as did the number of overlapping proteins^{cite80†15 ,cite82†16 ,cite83†17 }.\nL91: In a recent large study, the analyses were based mainly on indirect comparisons of different individuals enroled in the UK Biobank and the deCODE study^{cite83†17 }. Furthermore, while most previous studies focused on the comparison of pQTLs, observational proteomic associations with phenotypes are also important for biomarker discovery and risk prediction of diseases.\nL92: Therefore, more studies are needed to directly compare different assay platforms in identical samples that involve larger sample sizes, more overlapping proteins, and more comprehensive analyses from both genetic and phenotypic perspectives.\nL93: We present direct comparative analyses of 2168 proteins measured by both the Olink Explore 3072 (~3000 protein targets) and the SomaScan Assay v4.1 (~7000 protein targets) platforms in 3976 participants from an ischaemic heart disease (IHD) case-subcohort study within the China Kadoorie Biobank (CKB).\nL94: The main aims of this study were to: (i) examine the correlations between plasma protein levels measured by the two platforms; (ii) compare the pQTLs identified in genome-wide association studies (GWAS); (iii) compare the associations of proteins with different traits (e.g. adiposity) and disease risks (e.g. IHD); and (iv) compare the performance of proteins for prediction of IHD risk, separately and in combination with conventional risk factors.\nL95: ## Results\nL96: \nL97: Of the 3976 participants in the CKB proteomics study (1951 incident IHD cases and 2025 subcohort participants), 53.7% were female and the mean (SD) age at baseline was 57.3 (11.7) years and the mean (SD) BMI was 23.9 (3.6) kg/m^{2}. At sample collection, the mean ambient temperature was 15.7 °C (SD 10.5), and the mean time since last meal was 4.7 (SD 4.7) hours. Further details of participant characteristics by case-subcohort ascertainment are shown in Table cite84†1 .\nL98: Table 1 Baseline characteristics of IHD cases and subcohort participants who had no prior history of CVD at baseline\nL99: As shown in Fig. cite85†1 , after excluding duplicated reagents and reagents without corresponding UniProt IDs, there were 2923 reagents (mapped to 2923 UniProt IDs) included in the Olink Explore 3072 platform, and 7301 SOMAmers (mapped to 6397 UniProt IDs) included in SomaScan Assay v4.1. We identified 2749 Olink-SomaScan reagent pairs, which mapped to 2168 UniProt IDs (since some proteins were targeted by multiple SOMAmers and some SOMAmers targeted multiple proteins).\nL100: This included 1694 reagent pairs for which one unique SOMAmer was matched to one unique Olink reagent.\nL101: Fig. 1: Summary of study design, analytical approaches, and key findings.\nL102: \nL103: cite86†Image: Fig. 1: Summary of study design, analytical approaches, and key findings.†media.springernature.com L104: \nL105: cite87†Full size image L106: Main analyses were conducted on 1694 one-to-one matched Olink-SomaScan reagent pairs in 3976 CKB participants. Results were corrected for multiple testing using false discovery rate within each platform. Risk prediction models for IHD were built using conventional risk factors (age, sex, smoking, type 2 diabetes, systolic blood pressure, and waist circumference) and 1694 matched proteins. CKB China Kadoorie Biobank, IHD ischaemic heart disease, NRI net reclassification index.\nL107: ### Observational correlations and associated factors\nL108: The SomaScan data were provided in two versions, one with adaptive normalization by maximum likelihood (ANML; a procedure to normalise protein levels to an external reference to control for inter-sample variability) and one without ANML, and there were high correlations of protein levels (median Spearman’s rho = 0.82) between them.\nL109: The main analyses compared Olink with SomaScan-non-ANML data, as Olink did not include the ANML step, and there were higher correlations of Olink with SomaScan-non-ANML than with SomaScan-ANML data (Supplementary Fig. cite88†1 ). The results based on SomaScan-ANML are also provided in Supplementary Information.\nL110: For all 2749 Olink-SomaScan reagent pairs, we found a median Spearman’s rho of 0.29 between protein levels measured by two platforms (Supplementary Figs. cite88†1 , cite88†2 ; Supplementary Data cite89†1 ). Histograms suggest a bimodal distribution of Spearman’s rho, with a peak centred around 0 and another closer to 0.8. When restricting the analysis to the 1694 one-to-one matched pairs, we found a similar distribution of Spearman’s rho with a median of 0.26 (Fig. cite90†2a ; Supplementary Figs. cite88†3 , cite88†4 ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLarge-scale plasma proteomics comparisons through genetics and disease associations | Nature (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x)\nciteturn36view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x?error=cookies_not_supported&code=f57098a4-c007-4ecc-a887-ec42c028bb46; Total lines: 991\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://rnajc.ucsf.edu/sites/rnajc.ucsf.edu/files/nbt.3973_0.pdf)\nciteturn36view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://rnajc.ucsf.edu/sites/rnajc.ucsf.edu/files/nbt.3973_0.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 942\n"
